/* ============================================================================
one-sample-t-test.epher -- is the class mean really 75?

Tests whether the mean of the ten exam scores in `d` differs from the
claimed population mean 75 with the builtin one-sample t test
ttest(data, mu0) (guide 1.20), which prints the t statistic and the
two-sided p-value. The t statistic uses the sample standard deviation
(n - 1) under the mean, here about 9.9113. The test prints its result
as a display string; the lines above it show the pieces the statistic
is built from.

Run it from a terminal:
  Linux (deb, rpm):  epher /usr/lib/epher/scripts/statistics/tests/one-sample-t-test.epher
  Windows:           epher "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\epher\scripts\statistics\tests\one-sample-t-test.epher"
  macOS:             epher /Applications/epher.app/Contents/Resources/scripts/statistics/tests/one-sample-t-test.epher
In the REPL:             load one of the paths above
Web/desktop:             paste the whole file into the entry, press Enter
Everything also works line by line in the TUI.

The knob to edit is `mu0` (the claimed mean) below, or `d` for other
data. Everything above the results section is machinery you can leave
alone.
============================================================================ */

// ---- the knobs -----------------------------------------------------------------
const d = {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}   // exam scores, 10 students
const mu0 = 75                                       // the claimed population mean

// ---- helpers: the pieces of the t statistic --------------------------------------
def sds() = sqrt(variance(d) * len(d) / (len(d) - 1)) // sample stdev, n - 1

// ---- what the test sees ------------------------------------------------------------
print("sample mean:", mean(d))
print("sample size:", len(d))
print("sample stdev (n - 1):", sds())

// ---- the test ------------------------------------------------------------------------
ttest(d, mu0)   // t = 2.0101, p = 0.0753: at the 5% level the data do not
                // firmly contradict the claimed mean of 75, though the
                // sample mean of 81.3 sits 6.3 points above it

/* ---- expected output ----
= {72, 85, 91, 64, 88, 79, 83, 95, 70, 86}
= 75
= sample mean: 81.3
= sample size: 10
= sample stdev (n - 1): 9.91127304302
= t = 2.0101, p = 0.0753
*/